WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.360
Hello, welcome back, I m Dom and this is&nbsp;
the Map of Black Holes which isn t a map&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.560
of where all the black holes are in space, it&nbsp;
s a concept map of the subject of black holes:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.560
laying out our current knowledge of them,&nbsp;
the evidence for their existence, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.760
many outstanding mysteries still to be solved;&nbsp;
they are very strange and fascinating indeed.
&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.360
I find concept maps are really useful to give&nbsp;
you a good overall idea of a field of research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.080
so here s the current state of&nbsp;
knowledge about black holes.
&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.640
On Earth, getting into space in a space rocket&nbsp;
is really hard, because we need to climb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.640
out of the Earth s gravity well. But in a way&nbsp;
we re lucky. If the Earth was just fifty percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.640
larger by diameter it would be impossible to get&nbsp;
into orbit with any of our current technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.800
This would mean no astronauts, no&nbsp;
satellites, no GPS or google Earth.
&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.080
So the stronger the gravity of a planet, the&nbsp;
higher the escape velocity you need, and black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.960
holes are the most extreme example of this where&nbsp;
the escape velocity exceeds the speed of light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.560
There is so much mass squeezed into such a small&nbsp;
volume that nothing can escape because nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.560
can travel faster than the speed of light Hence&nbsp;
the name: black hole, which weirdly comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.800
the black hole of calcutta, a notorious prison&nbsp;
where people went in but nobody ever left alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.840
Although this name, black hole, only caught&nbsp;
on in the late 1960s, before that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:31.200
referred to as dark stars, frozen stars&nbsp;
or gravitationally collapsed objects. 
&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.480
The original idea that black holes could&nbsp;
exist came from our understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.640
Einstein's theory of relativity published in&nbsp;
1915, where gravity is explained as the curvature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.800
of spacetime. The more dense an object is the&nbsp;
more it curves spacetime, and the more curvature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.480
there is the stronger the force of gravity.
In 1916 Karl Schwarzchild found a solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.880
to the field equations of relativity which&nbsp;
predicted a special distance from the black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.120
hole called the Schwarzchild radius,&nbsp;
but is also known as the event horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.800
beyond which nothing can escape. This event&nbsp;
horizon was long considered a mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.600
curiosity until the sixties and seventies&nbsp;
where theoretical developments and experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.800
evidence mounted up and people realised black&nbsp;
holes did actually exist in our universe.
&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.760
This is a common visualisation of the&nbsp;
warping of spacetime around a black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.800
although this is just a visual analogy. These&nbsp;
squiggly lines represent photons which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.547
actually travelling in straight lines. They&nbsp;
only look like they curve around because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.547 --> 00:02:39.440
are travelling through a curved spacetime. This&nbsp;
phenomenon is known as gravitational lensing.
&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.520
The inner horizon is a feature of a rotating&nbsp;
black hole. Rotating and non-rotating black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.840
holes have got distinct features which we ll&nbsp;
see when we look at their structures in a bit.
&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.840
At the centre of the black hole is a singularity&nbsp;
where all of the mass of the black hole is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:02.480
squeezed into an infinitely small region of&nbsp;
infinite density creating an infinite curvature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:09.200
of spacetime. Well, that s probably not actually&nbsp;
true. This is a strong indication we are pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:15.920
the theory into a realm that it s not built to&nbsp;
cover. Really we need a theory of quantum gravity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.760
and people think that if we could add the laws of&nbsp;
quantum mechanics to general relativity this could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.440
explain what is going on in the centre of a black&nbsp;
hole. A theory of quantum gravity is the holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.080
grail of theoretical physics, which is why black&nbsp;
holes are so fascinating, because they are one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.600
the few places in the universe where gravity&nbsp;
gets big enough to be as strong as the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.160
three fundamental forces and so general relativity&nbsp;
and quantum mechanics are both needed to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.960
what is going on inside black holes.
Another way to visualise the motion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.640
particles near a black hole is using space-time&nbsp;
diagrams. Far from a black hole, we see these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.840
squiggly lines travelling at 45 degrees which&nbsp;
represents light travelling at the speed of light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.600
Because this is the cosmic speed limit, anything&nbsp;
with mass will have trajectories at some angle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.640
in between these limiting cases which is&nbsp;
traditionally referred to as a light cone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.960
As you get closer and closer to the black hole&nbsp;
this light cone bends towards it due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:23.120
curvature of spacetime, and if you cross the event&nbsp;
horizon, all paths point inwards to the centre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.840
which means whatever direction you travel&nbsp;
in you are always moving towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.960
centre of the black hole: every possible&nbsp;
future ends up with you getting squished.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:42.720
It s like space and time switch roles. Outside&nbsp;
the event horizon time only goes forwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.320
but inside the only direction you can move&nbsp;
is forwards in towards the singularity.
&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.480
And it s called an event horizon because any event&nbsp;
that happens inside the black hole can never be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:02.640
seen by anyone outside. So it is literally the&nbsp;
horizon over which events can never be seen.
&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.840
Most black holes that we know of are&nbsp;
created from the remnants of dying stars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.200
When stars exhaust their fuel they perish in a&nbsp;
variety of dramatic ways depending on their mass.
&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.880
Stars who s cores have a mass less than 1.4&nbsp;
times the mass of the sun collapse into white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.440
dwarf stars. Above this limit, known as the&nbsp;
Chandrasekhar limit, stars explode in a violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.040
supernova and collapse into a neutron star. The&nbsp;
incredible pressure of all that matter in the core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:39.360
overcomes the electron s ability to repel each&nbsp;
other, known as electron degeneracy pressure, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.880
the electrons and protons join together to make&nbsp;
an incredibly dense star made of pure neutrons.
&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:51.280
Then above that the theory says that a&nbsp;
star with a mass in the core above 2.17&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.080
times the mass of the sun will collapse&nbsp;
into an even denser object: a black hole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.600
There is a possibility there are other stars&nbsp;
more dense than a neutron star and less dense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.520
than black holes made of some form of exotic&nbsp;
matter, perhaps quark stars or strange stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.640
but these are hypothetical and we&nbsp;
have no evidence of them so far.
&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.960
But exploding stars is only one mechanism for&nbsp;
black hole formation, there are giant black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.520
holes out there called supermassive black&nbsp;
holes with masses of millions of times to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:28.320
tens of billions of times the mass of the sun.&nbsp;
How they got so big is still an open question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.120
Did they grow bigger over time by absorbing other&nbsp;
matter and black holes? Did they form from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.200
universe s earliest massive stars? Or perhaps&nbsp;
they formed directly after the big bang from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.720
primordial gas collapsing in on itself? We don&nbsp;
t yet know and this is still active research. 
&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.480
At the small mass scale it is theoretically&nbsp;
possible to have very low mass black holes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.880
The key feature of a black hole is not so much&nbsp;
its mass, but the very high density of that mass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.760
and so some people have wondered if tiny black&nbsp;
holes might be created in particle collisions like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:08.160
in the large hadron collider at CERN. You might&nbsp;
remember a media scare that there might be a black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.480
hole created in Switzerland that gobbled up the&nbsp;
Earth. But this was total nonsense because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.320
get particles from space called cosmic rays with&nbsp;
way higher energies than anything we can create on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.680
Earth and anything that was created at CERN. And&nbsp;
they don t create black holes in the atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:30.800
so there is absolutely no evidence that&nbsp;
miniature black holes are created from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.960
particle collisions so perhaps there is a lower&nbsp;
mass limit to black holes we don t know about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.480
this is another open question.
Here is how the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.720
masses of black holes are typically&nbsp;
classified according to mass and size:
&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.440
Micro-black holes would have a mass up to the mass&nbsp;
of the moon, but would be tiny, having a radius of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:55.760
their event horizons of 0.1 millimetres or less.
Stellar black holes have approximately ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.360
times the mass of the sun and are&nbsp;
about thirty kilometres in radius.
&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.920
Intermediate black holes are about a&nbsp;
thousand times the mass of the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.840
and are a similar size to the Earth with&nbsp;
a radius of about a thousand kilometres.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.480
Although the Earth actually has a radius of 6&nbsp;
thousand so I kind of got this picture wrong.
&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:18.560
Supermassive black holes have masses from a&nbsp;
hundred thousand times the mass of the sun to tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.920
of billions of solar masses and are so big they&nbsp;
get as big as four hundred astronomical units.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:29.040
For context, one astronomical unit is&nbsp;
the distance from the earth to the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.720
so four hundred astronomical units is&nbsp;
about ten times the orbit of pluto. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:09:27.840
is insane to imagine a black hole that big.
Let s take a closer look at the anatomy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:33.120
black holes. We ve already talked about the event&nbsp;
horizon and singularity, but there are many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.960
interesting features we need to talk about.
Near a black hole clocks run slow. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.800
the effect of the highly curved spacetime which&nbsp;
causes time dilation. This means the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.520
of someone who is falling into a black hole&nbsp;
would be quite different to someone watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.960
them fall in. If you travelled over the event&nbsp;
horizon, you wouldn t notice anything. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.680
depending on the size of the black hole, it wouldn&nbsp;
t be long until you got completely spaghettified.
&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:07.680
If, however, you watched someone falling into&nbsp;
a black hole, they would steadily move in slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:13.360
motion as their local clock slowed down,&nbsp;
and get more and more red shifted until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.320
they looked like a red smudge on the event&nbsp;
horizon that gradually faded out of existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.520
as the wavelength of light they&nbsp;
emit got longer and longer. 
&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.280
Then we have the singularity which I ve already&nbsp;
talked about, but basically it s a region of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.760
infinite curvature, where all the mass is squeezed&nbsp;
into zero volume with infinite density. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:39.280
implies a breakdown of general relativity, and&nbsp;
really a theory of quantum gravity is needed.
&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:44.880
Some more important features of a black&nbsp;
hole. Outside the black hole at 3 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:49.920
the Schwarzchild radius is the innermost&nbsp;
stable circular orbit which is the minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.640
distance a test particle could orbit&nbsp;
the black hole in a circle and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:00.320
marks the inner edge of an accretion disk&nbsp;
of infalling matter around a black hole.
&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:05.440
And at 1.5 the Schwarzchild radius is the&nbsp;
photon sphere, which is the only possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.840
circular orbit for a massless particle, and if&nbsp;
you sat here because photons are going all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:15.360
way around the black hole you could look at the&nbsp;
back of your own head. It also marks the closest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:21.200
distance any elliptical orbit of any kind of&nbsp;
matter can get. If anything travels below this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:27.840
it ll either get slingshotted out of the black&nbsp;
hole s gravity, or eventually spiral in.
&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.160
Also black holes give off Hawking&nbsp;
radiation from the event horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.640
although this is very very faint, and is&nbsp;
impossible for us to detect this unless we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:42.260
somehow get very very close to a black hole.
It s worth noting here that black holes don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:48.080
t have any special suction powers where they&nbsp;
go around the galaxy hoovering everything up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:54.800
The gravity of a black hole is the same shape&nbsp;
as any other massive body so, at a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:00.160
being around a black hole would feel just like&nbsp;
being around any other star with the same mass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:05.280
gravitationally speaking. They re special&nbsp;
because they are so dense, so when you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:09.840
close in to them you experience a way higher&nbsp;
gravity than you could get anywhere else.
&nbsp;

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.280
This picture only applies&nbsp;
to non-rotating black holes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:19.760
the picture for a rotating black hole is&nbsp;
a little more complicated as shown here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:26.400
Now you may wonder if you ever get non-rotating&nbsp;
black holes as everything in the universe is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:32.000
spinning in some way. But theoretically you can&nbsp;
get non-rotating black holes because Hawking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:36.720
radiation takes away some of the angular&nbsp;
momentum from the black hole, which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:41.680
gradually slow down their spin, but it would&nbsp;
take a very very long time for this to happen.
&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:49.040
Non-rotating black holes are spherical,&nbsp;
rotating black holes are a squashed oval shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.920
Also the singularity in the centre is&nbsp;
no longer a point, but a singularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:59.680
ring. And they have a region outside the event&nbsp;
horizon called an ergosphere which is a region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:03.680
where it would be impossible to stand still&nbsp;
because the rotation of the black hole drags&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:10.400
spacetime around it in a process called frame&nbsp;
dragging, kind of like a whirlpool of spacetime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:15.760
And the frame dragging is so fast inside the&nbsp;
ergosphere you would have to travel faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:21.520
than the speed of light just to stand still. So&nbsp;
you can move in the ergosphere, but only in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:27.200
direction of the rotation of the black hole.
At the edge of the ergosphere is the innermost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:34.240
stable orbit, the minimum distance to the black&nbsp;
hole a particle can maintain a stable orbit.
&nbsp;

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:38.480
Many real black holes are surrounded by an&nbsp;
accretion disk, a cloud of material that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:43.360
falling into the black hole and generating loads&nbsp;
of heat and energy as the particles speed up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:48.880
crash into each other. Accretion disks are&nbsp;
very bright sources of x-ray radiation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:54.480
high energy particles which can fly out of the&nbsp;
ergosphere with way more energy than they entered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.040
stealing angular momentum from the black&nbsp;
hole as they do so using it as a slingshot.
&nbsp;

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:05.280
Finally, the incredible gravity around a&nbsp;
black hole leads to interesting gravitational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:09.840
lensing effects on the accretion disk&nbsp;
resulting in this familiar image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.320
In reality the accretion disk&nbsp;
is actually a pancake shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:19.120
but we can see the accretion disk that would&nbsp;
be normally hidden behind the black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:24.480
because the light from it which travels upwards&nbsp;
or downwards is bent around the black hole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.600
and comes towards us. So it looks like there is&nbsp;
an accretion disk above and below the black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.240
but we are actually seeing the top and bottom of&nbsp;
the accretion disk that s behind the black hole.
&nbsp;

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:40.240
I ve been talking for a long time, but&nbsp;
how do we know black holes actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:46.000
exist? Well over the last 50 years we ve&nbsp;
built up a large body of observational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:51.440
evidence from many different techniques.
The first was from x-ray astronomy as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:58.000
radiation from the accretion disk is mostly x-ray&nbsp;
radiation. The process that produces this x-ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:04.080
radiation in the accretion disk is one of the&nbsp;
most efficient energy producing processes known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:11.920
where the spiralling material has up to 40% of its&nbsp;
rest mass converted into energy. To understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:17.600
hugely remarkable this is we can compare this&nbsp;
to the nuclear fusion process that powers the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:25.840
sun and all stars. Here only 0.7% of the rest&nbsp;
mass is converted to energy, way less than 40%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:31.600
So the accretion disk is nearly sixty&nbsp;
times more violent than a burning star.
&nbsp;

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:38.320
The very first black hole that was discovered&nbsp;
was discovered this way in 1971 and is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:43.040
Cygnus X-1. Since then we have discovered&nbsp;
around a hundred more, although this is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:47.520
a tiny sample of the number of black holes&nbsp;
that are thought to exist in our galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:52.240
and every single galaxy is thought to have&nbsp;
a supermassive black hole in its centre.
&nbsp;

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:57.600
Often accretion disks are accompanied&nbsp;
by relativistic jets, which emit even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:02.160
higher energy particles and radiation than&nbsp;
from the accretion disk. The mechanism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:08.320
creates these jests is not currently known.
We see many high energy sources in space, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:13.360
we think many of them are caused by the accretion&nbsp;
of matter into black holes. These include active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:18.560
galactic nuclei, and quasars which are thought&nbsp;
to be the accretion disks of supermassive black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.840
holes, and also ultraluminous x-ray sources&nbsp;
thought to be from intermediate mass black holes.
&nbsp;

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.760
Also things called x-ray binaries most&nbsp;
likely consist of a normal star and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:35.840
black hole orbiting each other while the black&nbsp;
hole gradually sucks away matter from the star.
&nbsp;

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:40.880
And short lived streamers are thought to be stars&nbsp;
which shine incredibly brightly as they are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:46.560
swallowed up by a black hole before disappearing.
An entirely different line of evidence comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:51.840
from the centre of our own galaxy by&nbsp;
tracking the orbits of about 100 stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:56.960
which have been observed to be orbiting around an&nbsp;
invisible massive object called Sagittarius A*.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:04.000
By tracing the orbits and measuring the velocities&nbsp;
of the orbiting stars astrophysicists have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:10.960
able to calculate the mass of the central body&nbsp;
to be a humongous 4.3 million times the mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:18.000
of the sun, but this mass is confined to&nbsp;
a region of less than 0.002 light years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:26.160
signs that it must be a supermassive black hole.
Even more recently in 2019 the first direct image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:30.240
of the accretion disk around a black&nbsp;
hole was constructed from the radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:36.240
waves emitted by the galactic centre of the&nbsp;
Messier 87 galaxy, and it had the features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:42.000
of an accretion disk that we expected, if a&nbsp;
little blurry. But constructing this image was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:47.200
a feat of radio astronomy and was achieved by&nbsp;
combining the signals from 8 radio telescopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:52.320
all around the world to create a virtual telescope&nbsp;
the size of the Earth; an amazing achievement.
&nbsp;

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:58.400
Just as incredible was the detection&nbsp;
of gravitational waves by LIGO in 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:05.280
which uses finely tuned lasers in a giant L shape&nbsp;
to detect the miniscule changes of distance caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:11.200
by the rippling of spacetime when gravitational&nbsp;
waves passed through the Earth. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:16.400
gravitational waves they detected were created&nbsp;
by a pair of stellar mass black holes spiralling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:22.480
into each other and finally merging into a larger&nbsp;
black hole, and converting around 5% of their mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.840
into gravitational waves. And since then LIGO&nbsp;
and VIRGO have detected many more collisions.
&nbsp;

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:34.640
Finally there is a proposed method called&nbsp;
microlensing which has been seen many times for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:40.000
stars, but not as far as we know for black holes.&nbsp;
I ve talked about how the strong gravitational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:45.520
field around a black hole causes a lensing effect.&nbsp;
The idea is we could use this to detect black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:51.120
holes when they pass in front of a star. We ve&nbsp;
seen things like supernovae be gravitationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:56.160
lensed by massive objects like entire galaxies.&nbsp;
But if we found a binary system where a star is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.160 --> 00:19:01.200
orbiting a black hole we could potentially see&nbsp;
the radiation from the star being bent towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:06.800
us as it passes behind the black hole which would&nbsp;
give us information about the black hole it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:11.680
orbiting, and would be a really cool technique&nbsp;
for the future of black hole observations.
&nbsp;

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:19.280
Let s take a quick look at the theoretical&nbsp;
understanding of black holes which started in 1916&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:25.360
but was really fleshed out in the 60s and 70s when&nbsp;
black holes were taken seriously as real physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:30.400
objects, not just mathematical curiosities.
Here is the equation for the radius of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:34.400
non-rotating black hole, which gives a&nbsp;
simple relationship with its mass where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:38.800
G is the gravitational constant and c is&nbsp;
the speed of light. But this gets more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:44.960
complicated if the black hole has spin or charge.
The general description of black holes, developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:49.840
in the 60 s is called the no hair theorem and&nbsp;
states that a stationary black hole can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:55.920
completely described by just three parameters:&nbsp;
mass, angular momentum and electric charge.
&nbsp;

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:01.120
Then in the 70s a theory of black hole&nbsp;
thermodynamics was developed which describes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:07.440
links between black hole mass and energy, how the&nbsp;
surface area of a black hole relates it s entropy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.160
and how the surface gravity&nbsp;
relates to temperature. 
&nbsp;

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:16.080
Then Stephen Hawking applied quantum field&nbsp;
theory to black holes and showed that black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:21.760
holes radiate Hawking radiation energy from the&nbsp;
event horizon with a characteristic black body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:27.040
spectrum where the temperature is proportional&nbsp;
to the surface gravity of the black hole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:33.120
So black holes will gradually lose their mass&nbsp;
over time by radiating away Hawking radiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.720
but this evaporation is slowest for the largest&nbsp;
black holes and fastest for the smallest black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:44.560
holes. Which means that micro-black holes would&nbsp;
be short-lived, but supermassive black holes will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:52.400
live for over 10^100 years which is a ludicrous&nbsp;
amount of time and those will be the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:57.360
objects that will exist before the heat&nbsp;
death of the universe. What is going on?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:04.320
Oh it s just a car.
This all gives you a good idea about what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:10.320
think we understand about black holes, but there&nbsp;
are many outstanding mysteries which we know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:14.960
and these all seem to boil down to our&nbsp;
lack of a theory of quantum gravity.
&nbsp;

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:18.640
I ve already mentioned how singularities&nbsp;
probably don t exist because they cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:24.400
be infinitely small with infinite density. So&nbsp;
there s a question: what actually happens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:31.600
matter at the singularity? Unfortunately if we&nbsp;
ever found out experimentally, because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:38.640
event horizon we couldn t ever get the results&nbsp;
out to anyone which is a bit of an issue to ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:46.720
figure out what s going on in the singularity.
Also, in the description of spinning or charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:52.480
black holes there is a hypothetical possibility&nbsp;
of them containing an unstable wormhole which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:56.480
would let you leave the black hole but&nbsp;
in a completely different space time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:02.560
They also have regions inside them where it&nbsp;
would be possible to travel to your own past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:08.320
along closed timelike curves which creates one&nbsp;
of those time-travel grandfather paradoxes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:12.880
But, although these ideas are tantalizing&nbsp;
from a science fiction perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:17.200
both of these will probably go away with a&nbsp;
proper quantum description of black holes.
&nbsp;

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:21.920
There are other theoretical objects which have&nbsp;
been conjectured, but there is no evidence for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:26.000
them yet. These include stars somewhere in&nbsp;
between neutron stars and black holes but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:32.560
these are all highly speculative. Aslo white&nbsp;
holes which are like black holes but where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:37.680
event horizon goes the other way. Information&nbsp;
can come out, but nothing can ever go in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:44.000
And finally naked singularities which are&nbsp;
singularities which could be observed because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:49.440
don t have an event horizon, but these are thought&nbsp;
to be unphysical and so not actually possible.
&nbsp;

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:53.840
But these aren t as interesting&nbsp;
as the real theoretical puzzles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:58.800
First of all is the holographic principle,&nbsp;
which is complicated to explain, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:04.080
general gist of it is that black holes might&nbsp;
be telling us the whole universe is a hologram.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:10.000
This is a strange result of the entropy of a black&nbsp;
hole. In every other thing in the universe entropy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:15.040
scales with the volume of that thing, but in&nbsp;
black holes it scales with the surface area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:19.840
and this implies that anything that&nbsp;
happens within a volume of spacetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:27.520
can be described by data on the surface of that&nbsp;
volume, so, we re all a hologram. But, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:32.160
we d need a theory of quantum gravity to&nbsp;
properly calculate the entropy of a black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:37.040
so this is still active work.
Finally we have one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:43.120
unsolved mysteries of black holes, what happens&nbsp;
to the information that falls into them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:50.240
Now because black holes are defined by only three&nbsp;
parameters: mass, charge and angular momentum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:56.400
any other information about objects that&nbsp;
fall into the black hole appears to be erased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:02.080
from the universe. But the thing is this isn t&nbsp;
allowed by a fundamental law of quantum mechanics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:08.720
called unitarity. So for example, an electron&nbsp;
has a load of quantum numbers associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:14.320
it that define it like lepton number, but&nbsp;
if an electron falls into a black hole this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:18.720
information is lost because the black hole only&nbsp;
preserves mass, charge, and angular momentum.
&nbsp;

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:25.920
So one attempt to explain this is a&nbsp;
theory called complementarity which says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:30.160
the information does actually come&nbsp;
back out again, but in the Hawking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:36.240
radiation. The trouble was this was shown to&nbsp;
fail by a further paradox called the firewall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:41.520
paradox. Now the details of all these things&nbsp;
are complicated but the end result is that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:47.600
resolve all these paradoxes about information we&nbsp;
may need to give up one of the fundamental rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:53.680
in the laws of physics, either: Einstein s&nbsp;
equivalence principle, unitarity in quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.680 --> 00:25:02.400
mechanics, or local quantum field theory.
But overall black holes are fascinating objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:08.400
to study as they are the most extreme environments&nbsp;
in the universe where gravity and quantum physics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:16.400
meet and their very existence seems to break our&nbsp;
fundamental theories of physics. But by studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:21.600
them the hope is we may actually uncover some&nbsp;
subtle clues that could help guide us to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:27.360
next level of understanding so perhaps black&nbsp;
holes are actually the experimental key to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:35.200
developing a theory of quantum gravity, or perhaps&nbsp;
we ll just be a confused as we are now, forever.

